module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.set_element_kind_interpreted_native_divergence

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

// The interpreted and native routes do not agree about WHICH ELEMENT KINDS a set has. The failure is a
// refusal, so it is not silent -- but the refusal names the wrong cause, and its sibling cluster
// (maps) already has a refusal that names the right one.
data set_element_kind_interpreted_native_divergence: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "set_element_kind_interpreted_native_divergence" as NonEmptyStr,

  // Read in source. Below the ladder: a non-String element today reaches a refusal, but the wrong one.
  // Ceiling judgement is MINE, stated as a judgement: the misattribution is preventable by one
  // generalized grounding path, not by anything structural, hence MechanicallyPreventable.
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundBelowTheLadder,
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the existing collection-primitive grounding is generalized to cover map AND set shapes, with a refusal message that names the seam and the element kind, so a non-String set element is refused as an unrealized element kind rather than reported as a missing function",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "element kinds differ per route. NATIVE: src/v1/stage0/src/v1_rt.rs:1037 `set_contains<T: Ord, S: AsRef<BTreeSet<T>>>` answers membership for ANY element type that is Ord, so Set<Int>, Set<UInt32> and Set<String> are all realized emitted. INTERPRETED: the `arm \"free_call.set_contains\"` arm of `eval_builtin` (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs) is a single match arm, `[Value::Set(s), Value::Str(k)] => Ok(Some(Value::Bool(s.contains(k.as_ref()))))`, with `_ => Ok(None)` -- String elements only; every other element kind reports \"not handled\" rather than answering or refusing. So `set_contains(Set<Int>, Int)` is a valid program that builds and runs emitted and is unimplemented interpreted, which is a parity gap on the project's central claim, invisible to every existing test because all consumers are Set<String>.",

    "WHERE THE Ok(None) GOES, AND WHY THE REFUSAL MISATTRIBUTES THE CAUSE. The general call site is the builtin-tier call in `eval_call` (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs): `if let Some(result) = eval_builtin(&func_name, &args, ctx)? { return Ok(result); }`, so Ok(None) does not return: evaluation FALLS THROUGH the builtin tier, past the `v1_bridge_dispatch` macro arms and the `v1_native_intercept_dispatch` macro arms, to the module free-function table via `ctx.lookup_fn_from(&func_name, file)`. BEFORE THIS DRAFT, the set cluster had no .dag body at all (no `fn empty_set`, `fn set_insert` or `fn set_contains` anywhere under src/v2/std/ or dag/std/; the names were enumerated only as builtins in std.primitives builtin_registry_surface_names), so that lookup missed and the module tier's `ok_or_else` raised `Err(InterpError::NoSuchFunction { name })`. RUNG: a refusal, not silent wrongness -- a typed error is raised, so nothing wrong is returned. THE DEFECT IS THE DIAGNOSTIC: the interpreter says \"no such function: set_contains\" for a call that is valid emitted for any Ord element and valid interpreted for String keys. Nothing in that message mentions element kind, so the natural (and wrong) theory is a missing import or a missing roster row. The cause is the element kind; the message reports existence.",

    "THE SIBLING CLUSTER GOT A LOUD WRAPPER AND SET DID NOT. Map primitives pass through `try_v2_std_collection_primitive_grounding` (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs), which turns the SAME Ok(None) into a purpose-built `Err(InterpError::TypeError { msg: \"<fn> is a v2 std collection map primitive grounded to the Rust HostRealizedSeam; the bridge dispatch and builtin tier declined it, so this is native map primitive refused this argument shape (host misconfiguration, or a non-native map carrier reached a HostRealizedSeam)\" })` in that arm's `Ok(None)` branch -- a message that names the seam and the shape. That path is gated on `is_v2_std_collection_grounded_fn` and its arm list (`v1_collection_grounding_arms!`) contains map names only (empty_map, map_insert, ...); there is no set analogue anywhere in the file. So map_insert with an unrepresentable key is refused AS A SHAPE PROBLEM AT A SEAM, while set_contains with an unrepresentable element is reported AS A MISSING FUNCTION. Same failure class, two qualities of refusal, and the weaker one is on the cluster being rostered. This asymmetry is the evidence that the missing wrapper is an omission rather than a considered design.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, AT CAPABILITY GRAIN, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. The fix is to GENERALIZE the existing map grounding into one collection-primitive grounding covering map AND set shapes, per the root-generalization rule -- NOT to add a copied set twin beside it, which would leave one rule with two leaves and guarantee they drift. A copied twin is a mitigation, not a climb, and must not be recorded as moving this row. That change is v1-seed interpreter work and needs the v1 purpose test; it is not part of the draft that files this row. OWN CONTROL OBLIGATION: the companion control in the roster sweep asserts the error KIND (NoSuchFunction) for a Set<Int> membership and carries the message text as evidence only. When the generalized grounding lands, the kind changes and the control goes red on purpose, forcing a conscious update rather than a silent pass -- a control that asserted only today's message text would keep passing for the wrong reason.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.primitives", decl_name: "builtin_registry_surface_names", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "finite_power_set_templates", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ,

    "WHAT THIS ROW DOES NOT CLAIM, AND THE WORLD IT SHIPS INTO. NOT CLAIMED: that interpreter name resolution is sound in general, or that this row's fix closes anything about it. lookup_fn_from (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs) answers a site's own module first, then explicitly imported names, then FALLS THROUGH to a GLOBAL bare-name slot: fn_nodes, which \"holds one bare slot per name and one qualified slot per declaration\" (:6540, bare keys written :7154-7160). Any module-level fn is therefore reachable from EVERY module, whether or not the caller imports the name -- reachability the checker's bare-name cut (#15247) does not cover, because checker and interpreter are separate resolvers. This row claims only the set-cluster element-kind gap; the shared-slot reachability is recorded here so nobody reads the generalized grounding as having addressed it, which it does not. CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS DRAFT'S OWN BODIES, and the reason the earlier receipt above is scoped to BEFORE: once std.algebra declares fn set_contains and fn set_contains_primitive_delegate (dag/std/algebra.dag, the delegate body calling itself), the `lookup_fn_from` tier no longer MISSES -- it FINDS those bodies and the delegate recurses into itself. For set_contains(Set<Int>, Int) the refusal becomes InterpError::CallDepthExceeded { limit, at } after CALL_DEPTH_LIMIT = 100_000 frames, whose message offers \"a divergent chain, or a host seam whose realization this binary lacks\". Bounded and loud, never a host stack overflow -- so the rung is UNCHANGED (still a refusal naming the wrong cause, still MechanicallyPreventable) and only the diagnostic evidence moved: NoSuchFunction before the bodies, CallDepthExceeded after them. The bodies must not ship without the generalized grounding; that is why they are one change, not two.",

    "THE POPULATION THE HAZARD APPLIES TO, AND WHY IT IS LATENT RATHER THAN ACTIVE. The set cluster\u2019s real consumers are v1 seed-compiler call sites, read at the cited lines: src/v1/04_infer.dag:562 and :572 (set_contains(recursive_type_set, ...)), :19879 (set_insert(acc, name)), :19969 and :19972 (the EnumVariantShapeSets folds), :20087 (set_insert(inner, recursive_name)); src/v1/complexity.dag:3345, :3348 and :4825; src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag:1017, :1677 and :1731. Every one of them is String-kinded on the current tree: the elements are entry.name, member, qualified, name, leaf, type_name, value_type and dep, and src/v1/04_infer.dag:19953 declares type EnumVariantShapeSets { fielded: Set<String>  positional_payload: Set<String> }. Nothing therefore reaches the interpreted fall-through today, so the hazard is LATENT and becomes active exactly when a set with a non-String element is built or queried. src/v2/workflow/claim_coverage_census_s1.dag, cited by earlier drafts of this row, was retired on main by 4b70b5fe58c (#15558) and is no longer a consumer.",

    "WHICH SURFACES CAN FALL THROUGH, AND A TELEMETRY CONSEQUENCE STATED WITHOUT CLAIMING IMPACT. The three builtins are NOT symmetric. The arm \"free_call.empty_set\" returns Ok(Some(Value::Set(...))) unconditionally, so an empty_set() call can never reach a module body and a .dag empty_set body is inert to the interpreted route. set_insert and set_contains are each [Value::Set(s), Value::Str(k)] with _ => Ok(None), so the hazard population is exactly the pair {set_insert, set_contains} crossed with a non-String element, and a generalized grounding must cover both, not membership alone. Those builtin arms also increment mutation counters (counters.set_insert_calls and counters.set_insert_items_copied in src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs), which a fall-through into a .dag body would not, so the two routes differ in telemetry as well as in behaviour. UNVERIFIED, AND LEFT UNVERIFIED ON PURPOSE: whether any reader consumes those counters. A read of src/v1/stage0/tests and crates/*/tests found no reference to set_insert at all, and within src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs the names appear only at increment sites and in a Display impl, so NO IMPACT IS CLAIMED -- this records a property of the two routes, not a defect with an observed victim."    "THE GATE THIS ROW ASKED FOR IS IN THE TREE AS OF THIS CHANGE, AND THE RUNG IS NOT MOVED HERE. The trigger_capability above -- one generalized collection grounding whose refusal names the seam and the element kind -- is realized by this change: the dispatch site, its roster rows, the emitted lookup and the host predicate no longer spell `map` (arm identities `collection_grounding.*`, site key `try_v2_std_collection_primitive_grounding`, host predicate `is_v2_std_collection_grounded_fn`, const `STD_COLLECTION_GROUNDED_FNS`), and the set cluster joins the same site through six roster rows and three macro arms for `empty_set`, `set_insert` and `set_contains` with their `*_primitive_delegate` spellings. A non-String element therefore reaches `Ok(None)` INSIDE the gate, where it is now converted into `InterpError::TypeError` naming the seam, the primitive and the argument kinds, instead of falling through to the module body that calls back into the same primitive. BUILT AT THIS COMMIT AND UNEXECUTED: the gate is in the tree at the head carrying this receipt (its sha is named in the pull request body, since a commit cannot contain its own sha), and the controls that would establish a rung have not run. Per DESIGN section 4b the reported rung is the one established by EXECUTED evidence -- a discriminating RED refused on the real route plus a positive control -- so a realized capability does not move it. No execution verdict is claimed here. The three controls registered in src/v2/workflow/roster_import_sweep.dag (`set-cluster-*-refuses-without-occurrences`) exercise the site-gated ROSTER refusal, not this host refusal; the native and interpreted per-route outcomes for `Set<Int>` stay stated expectations until a run observes them; and the emitted mirror is regenerated by the registered producer on heal, not authored here. The rung stays `FoundBelowTheLadder` with `blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt` -- a row does not promote itself, and the ceiling judgement goes back to the owner with these receipts.",
],
}
